A||N||11||GT-maniac||Spoilers on family sedans||||1||
Z||000000||GT-maniac||06-07-2001||10:48 PM||||Is it just me or is the whole spoiler thing getting way out of hand ?. The other day I saw a spoiler on a chevy lumina, a lumina !.<BR>You can't get much more boring than that, and now I'm seeing vestigial spoilers on every kind of car made today. I don't know I was kind of thinking that spoilers belong on sports cars, or at least sport versions of cars, not on 4 door grocery getters. It's kind of like the salesman was telling them yes your being practical when you buy this Toyota Prius, but you can still pretend you have a sporty side with this go fast spoiler tacked on the back here. Now I have nothing against spoilers, I have one myself which I'm going to put on someday. I just think they look ridiculous on cars with no pretensions of sportiness. I'll get off my soapbox now as I was just wondering if anyone else agrees with this view. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||mblackwell||06-07-2001||10:58 PM||||They look really dumb on minivans.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DecadenceR||06-07-2001||11:29 PM||||I used to sell cars for Mazda, and I had a guy that WOULD NOT buy the MPV (minivan) unless we added a spoiler. Which, by the way, that van comes with spoiler and a GFX package...||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierofrek||06-07-2001||11:35 PM||||LMAO, I got ya'll beat. right down the road is a "mom&pop" custom shop, their promotion car is a yugo, with a wing!!! lowered, and a system. LOL I shouldn't forget to mention the bumble bee exhaust LOL, but in all truthfulness, they are nice people. just a stupid car!||||reg||1
Z||000004||WampusCat||06-08-2001||12:09 AM||||Like it really has any functional use on a Fiero either.  Car and Driver Mag. did a test of several "sporty" cars for the Sep 98 issue with and without the wings or spoilers installed.  Basically, they had no effect on handling and the Mustang they tested had a few mph faster top speed without the spoiler.  This link <A HREF="http://www.caranddriver.com/xp/Caranddriver/features/1998/September/199809_feature_doair.xml?&page=1" TARGET=_blank>http://www.caranddriver.com/xp/Caranddriver/features/1998/September/199809_feature_doair.xml?&page=1</A>  has the main body of the article, but I couldn't find the sidebar with the test results on their website.||||reg||1
Z||000005||kslish||06-08-2001||03:20 AM||||WampusCat, actually the rear spoiler/wing on a Fiero does serve a purpose and is functional.  It cleans up the aerodynamics of the car (but it isn't there to provide downforce).<P>Here are some quotes taken from the 1985 Motor Trend road test of the '85 Fiero GT:<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>"Compared to a base model Fiero whose drag coefficient is a not-so-impressive .41, the addition of the (aero) facias and rocker panels reduces Cd to .37, and the optional wing brings it down to .35. A serious bite from the old aerodynamic drag."<P>"The reduction in drag coefficient we mentioned above, attributable to the wing, is possibly due to a reduction to a reduction in turbulance above the rear decklid."<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Having a vertical rear window like the Fiero isn't the greatest thing aerodynamically because the air sort of "tumbles" off the roof instead of flowing smoothly.  The Fiero wing is there to act as an airfoil to smooth the flow of air off of the rear of the car.  (Incidentally, the "tumbling" of the air off the roof also creates a slight vacuum that helps to suck hot engine comaprtment air out throught the decklid vents at speed).  <P>The Fiero's aerodynamics are actually a pretty cool thing when you sit down and look at it (I always knew my Physics degree would come in handy some day).  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>P.S.- The drag numbers listed above are for the Fiero GT notchback.  I've got the GT fastback's drag numbers buried somewhere in my Fiero literature collection.  If someone really wants to know them I'll see if I can locate them.<P>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR><A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> <p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||WampusCat||06-08-2001||08:37 AM||||Wow, GM actually chose function over style?  or was it a happy coincidence?  Cool beans, thanks for the info, Kslish.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  The C&D article noted that front air dams are much more effective for the average car driven at highway speeds that anything you can hang on the rear deck.  Gotta get that coupe nose off my GT and return it to aero style.<p>[This message has been edited by WampusCat (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||RossT||06-08-2001||08:47 AM||||What! you don't like my spoiler on my family car!!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image008_2.jpg"> <P>now you got me really mad.  I thought for sure that spoiler would kill a few ricers..||||reg||1
Z||000008||stsmithgt||06-08-2001||10:03 AM||||A lot of spoilers like that, especially on stationwagons were made to blow air down the back of the vehicle.  If you had an open rear window like some vehicles can, all your exhaust fume would be sucked into the car while going down the road.  The spoiler on the back reduces that problem.  Of course the fumes make it in while in traffic.  Ever see the back of a vehicle all dirty from road grime?  Spoilers that force the air down were to reduce that too.  <p>[This message has been edited by stsmithgt (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||06-08-2001||01:26 PM||||ya, spoilers have got out of hand. A Honda Civic, Ford Taurus.........LOL. Most cars spoilers do absolutly nothing unless your going over 150-160 mph. The Fiero case though does have a slight effect in cd. Its pretty big relative to cars size compared to most others. Most US cars spoilers are really to tie ropes on for rollerblading and snow sleding.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierojunky||06-08-2001||01:43 PM||||I have a 2001 Grand Am GT (4 door) with a spoiler and i think it looks sharp.  Also has ram air and dual exhaust.  And could probably toast my fiero.  <BR>EDIT:  TYPOS<p>[This message has been edited by fierojunky (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||kslish||06-08-2001||01:52 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WampusCat:<BR><B>Gotta get that coupe nose off my GT and return it to aero style.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Which coupe nose?  The 87-88 coupe (and Formula) nose is actually the most aerodynamic Fiero nose style.  With the aero GT/SE/Indy style close behind.  <P>The '84 through '86 coupe noses are pretty bad though, generates a lot of lift at high speed, definately a case of too much styling design over function (everything below the beltline is angled down and in, including the front air dam).  <P>The Fiero's rear wing was fortunately a result of more than just coinincidence...GM was after high speed stability when they designed the Indy aero package with the wing so the car could pace the Indy 500 comfortably.  <P>Here's another interesting Fiero aero fact:  The GT/SE/Indy aero rear facia exhaust outlets were designed to function as low pressure ducts around the exhaust tips that actually use the car's aerodynamics to help scavenge the exhaust.<P>Cool stuff indeed....<P>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> <p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||1
